UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4: Follow a story through time', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Readers separate event order (what happened) from telling order (how the writer presents events), and use time clues to spot a flashback that explains a goal.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nOrder these: brother holds the gate; Nia finds the envelope; Nia reads the moved-club notice. Which happened first, and which phrase signals a flashback?\n\nFEEDBACK\nFirst: finds the envelope. Flashback signal: Earlier that morning (return: Now). Goal → obstacle → response connects the map.\n\nCHECK\nTreating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMap goal, obstacle, response and outcome for an original four-event story that includes one signalled flashback. Number events in happened order, mark move-back and return clues, and write three sentences explaining how the opening affects a reader.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Readers separate event order (what happened) from telling order (how the writer presents events), and use time clues to spot a flashback that explains a goal.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Map goal, obstacle, response and outcome for an original four-event story that includes one signalled flashback. Number events in happened order, mark move-back and return clues, and write three sentences explaining how  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using event order on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order.. Correction: First: finds the envelope.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating event order on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with event order, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about event order with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for event order. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for event order. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","First: finds the envelope","Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Order these: brother holds the gate; Nia finds the envelope; Nia reads the moved-club notice. Which happened first, and which phrase signals a flashback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for event order; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Readers separate event order (what happened) from telling order (how the writer presents events), and use time clues to","First: finds the envelope","Careful use of telling order and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with event order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named telling order and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order","Name telling order and explain why it matters only, without linking it to event order","Apply event order to new material and name the telling order and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies event order with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries event order. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show event order and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order"],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses event order from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["First: finds the envelope","Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name telling order and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about event order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse telling order and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for event order?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about event order","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of event order on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about event order with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating event order on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using event order on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for event order. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with event order, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order.. Correction: First: finds the envelope.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise event order.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use event order with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify telling order and explain why it matters.","I can practise flashback independently on a new prompt.","I can notice goal needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Readers separate event order (what happened) from telling order (how the writer presents events), and use time clues to spot a flashback that explains a goal.","options":["Readers separate event order (what happened) from telling order (how the writer presents events), and use time clues to","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about event order with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn \"The Blue Envelope\", Nia stands at the library with five minutes left. Earlier that morning she found the invitation under the kitchen table. Now she follows a notice to the courtyard. Event order: find → bring → read notice → deliver. Telling order starts at the library, flashes back, then returns with Now.\n\nTRY FIRST\nOrder these: brother holds the gate; Nia finds the envelope; Nia reads the moved-club notice. Which happened first, and which phrase signals a flashback?\n\nFEEDBACK\nFirst: finds the envelope. Flashback signal: Earlier that morning (return: Now). Goal → obstacle → response connects the map.","explain":"Readers separate event order (what happened) from telling order (how the writer presents events), and use time clues to spot a flashback that explains a goal.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Treating the first paragraph as the earliest event confuses telling order with event order. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Event order contrasted with telling order for the envelope stor